  "findings": [
    {
      "description": "Path traversal in download_archive(): filename from request is joined directly to STORAGE_ROOT without validation. An attacker can supply '../' sequences or an absolute path (e.g. ../../etc/passwd or /etc/passwd) to read arbitrary files from the server. Confirmed by sast-scan AST_PATH_TRAVERSAL on line 8 (open call) and handle_download passes untrusted request.args.get('file') directly.",
      "category": "injection",
      "severity": "critical"
    },
    {
      "description": "Path traversal in list_user_files(): username is joined to STORAGE_ROOT and passed to os.listdir() without sanitization. A crafted username such as '..' can enumerate arbitrary directories on the host.",
      "category": "injection",
      "severity": "high"
    }
  ],
  "decision": "REJECT",
  "fix_description": "Yes, both findings are fixable. For download_archive(): validate that the resolved path is inside STORAGE_ROOT before opening the file, e.g. use os.path.realpath() and ensure the path starts with the real STORAGE_ROOT prefix, and reject names containing path separators, null bytes, or traversal sequences. For list_user_files(): sanitize username by rejecting empty, '.', '..', or names containing path separators before using os.listdir(); also apply the same containment check on realpath. Optionally use a canonical allowlist for filenames/usernames. The fix should be applied at the handle_download and list_user_files entry points, not just the helper functions, because request.args.get('file') and username come from untrusted user input.",
